<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lothar Franz von Schönborn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1655">1655</a>–<a href="/years/1729">1729</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lothar Franz von Schönborn was prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> from 1693 and archbishop-elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> from 1695 until his death. A great patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> architecture, he built the New Residence in Bamberg and the palaces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paide">Weissenstein</a> at Pommersfelden and Gaibach. As arch-chancellor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> he supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and advanced his family's power across the Franconian sees.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lothar-franz-von-schonborn.jpg" alt="Lothar Franz von Schönborn" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christian Schilbach · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kurf%C3%BCrst_Lothar_Franz_von_Sch%C3%B6nborn.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lothar Franz was born on 4 October 1655 in Steinheim am Main, now a suburb of Hanau, to Count Philipp Erwein von Schönborn and Maria Ursula von Greiffenclau-Vollraths. He and his elder brother Melchior Friedrich von Schönborn-Buchheim were nephews of Johann Philipp von Schönborn, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> from 1647 to 1673, and through their mother grand-nephews of Georg Friedrich von Greiffenklau, Archbishop of Mainz from 1626 to 1629. Through Melchior he was uncle of the next generation of the Schönborn-Buchheim branch, among them Johann Philipp Franz and Friedrich Karl.</p><p>He was educated at the Jesuit College in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aschaffenburg">Aschaffenburg</a>, became a canon of Würzburg Cathedral in 1665 and of Bamberg Cathedral in 1667, and received a prebend at Mainz Cathedral in 1674. He made his Grand Tour through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and spent his two years of preparation, from 1673 to 1675, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Holding to the emperor's side, he served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamberg">Bamberg</a> on diplomatic missions and as President of the Court Chamber. Elected Bishop of Bamberg in 1693, he was made coadjutor of Anselm Franz von Ingelheim in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> in September 1694 against the rival candidate Franz Ludwig von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-neuburg">Pfalz-Neuburg</a>, and succeeded him in 1695 despite the emperor's recommendation against him. He raised the taxation of the cathedral chapter and curtailed some of its rights. In 1707 he helped bring about the conversion of the Protestant Elizabeth Christina of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>.</p><p>In 1711 he secured the election of Charles VI as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> and crowned him in Frankfurt Cathedral. Charles rewarded him with 100,000 guldens, with which he began Schloss Weißenstein at Pommersfelden; he also built Favorite Palace near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> between 1700 and 1722.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1726, after Lothar Franz had sent troops against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii-rakoczi">Francis II Rákóczi</a>, Charles VI granted him Palanok Castle with Mukacheve, Chynadiyovo and 200 villages in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>, now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>; the estate was one of the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> and stayed in the family well into the 20th century. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> on 30 January 1729. Weißenstein, with the largest privately owned art gallery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, still belongs to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schonborn-family">Schönborn family</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lothar Franz von Schönborn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the baroque era <b>Lothar Franz von Schönborn</b> built the New Residence.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-bamberg">Prince-Bishopric of Bamberg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50,8.27&amp;year=1703" aria-label="Mainz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4740 424 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Lothar Franz von Schönborn"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5190 694h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5190 694h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50,8.27&amp;year=1703">What was here in 1703? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
